1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,955
You are nineteen years old, and in about one minute, you have

2
00:00:02,955 --> 00:00:05,910
to kill a man in front of the entire town.

3
00:00:06,209 --> 00:00:09,369
Five hundred people came to watch. Half of them will cheer for

4
00:00:09,369 --> 00:00:12,529
you. The other half will never touch your hand again.

5
00:00:12,830 --> 00:00:16,120
And the worst part? You never chose this job. Your father got

6
00:00:16,120 --> 00:00:19,410
it by accident. And now it's yours forever.

7
00:00:19,710 --> 00:00:22,483
Let's go back to how you got here. Technically this is the

8
00:00:22,483 --> 00:00:25,256
fifteen hundreds, not the Middle Ages. But nobody ever got sued for

9
00:00:25,256 --> 00:00:28,029
saying medieval.

10
00:00:28,329 --> 00:00:31,216
And don't worry. This is not one of those made up stories.

11
00:00:31,216 --> 00:00:34,103
The man you're about to become was real. He kept a diary.

12
00:00:34,103 --> 00:00:36,990
We'll get to that.

13
00:00:37,289 --> 00:00:41,495
You're born around fifteen fifty-four, in the town of Hof, in Bavaria.

14
00:00:41,495 --> 00:00:45,700
Your dad is a normal guy. He works in the woods.

15
00:00:46,000 --> 00:00:49,079
Then one day, a prince shows up who needs three prisoners hanged.

16
00:00:49,079 --> 00:00:52,160
And the town has no executioner.

17
00:00:52,460 --> 00:00:55,609
His name is Margrave Albrecht Alcibiades. Yes, that's a real name. He

18
00:00:55,609 --> 00:00:58,760
is famous mostly for starting wars, and he's not the kind of

19
00:00:58,760 --> 00:01:01,910
man you say no to.

20
00:01:02,210 --> 00:01:05,435
So the prince looks at the crowd, picks a random man, and

21
00:01:05,435 --> 00:01:08,659
orders him to do it. That man is your dad.

22
00:01:08,959 --> 00:01:11,185
Say no, and maybe it's your neck in the rope. So your

23
00:01:11,185 --> 00:01:13,409
dad says yes.

24
00:01:13,709 --> 00:01:18,829
And just like that, your whole family is ruined. In this world,

25
00:01:18,829 --> 00:01:23,950
the executioner is officially dishonourable. Not a bad person. Something worse. Unclean.

26
00:01:24,250 --> 00:01:28,193
Executioners in German lands were often expected to live apart, at the

27
00:01:28,193 --> 00:01:32,136
edge of town. People avoided touching them. Some even believed that just

28
00:01:32,136 --> 00:01:36,079
touching an executioner could make you dishonourable too.

29
00:01:36,379 --> 00:01:39,164
Think about that. You can catch shame like a cold. And your

30
00:01:39,164 --> 00:01:41,950
family is now patient zero.

31
00:01:42,250 --> 00:01:44,664
You grow up as the executioner's kid. Other children are told not

32
00:01:44,664 --> 00:01:47,079
to play with you.

33
00:01:47,379 --> 00:01:49,740
At church, your family sits apart.

34
00:01:50,040 --> 00:01:53,640
At the tavern, executioners often had their own seat or their own

35
00:01:53,640 --> 00:01:57,240
cup, so nobody would accidentally drink after them.

36
00:01:57,540 --> 00:02:01,745
You can't join a guild. You can't become a baker, a carpenter,

37
00:02:01,745 --> 00:02:05,950
or anything respectable. There is exactly one career open to you.

38
00:02:06,250 --> 00:02:09,619
Your dad's. Forever. And then your kids' too.

39
00:02:09,919 --> 00:02:13,789
Even marriage is a problem. Who marries an executioner's kid? Usually, another

40
00:02:13,789 --> 00:02:17,659
executioner's kid. Whole family dynasties of hangmen marrying each other, because nobody

41
00:02:17,659 --> 00:02:21,530
else would.

42
00:02:21,830 --> 00:02:25,680
So your dad trains you. You practice your sword swing on animals

43
00:02:25,680 --> 00:02:29,530
and pumpkins, because the real exam is a human being.

44
00:02:29,830 --> 00:02:34,180
You also learn something surprising. Anatomy. Bones, joints, muscles. An executioner has

45
00:02:34,180 --> 00:02:38,530
to know exactly how a body works.

46
00:02:38,830 --> 00:02:43,889
Partly to execute people quickly. And partly, let's be honest, because the

47
00:02:43,889 --> 00:02:48,949
job also includes torture. Here's the official interrogation room. Moving on.

48
00:02:49,250 --> 00:02:53,483
And then there are the side jobs. In many towns, the executioner

49
00:02:53,483 --> 00:02:57,716
also had to deal with the jobs nobody else would touch. Removing

50
00:02:57,716 --> 00:03:01,949
dead animals. Cleaning out latrines. Chasing away stray dogs.

51
00:03:02,250 --> 00:03:06,224
So your career path is: kill criminals, torture suspects, and pick up

52
00:03:06,224 --> 00:03:10,199
dead horses. LinkedIn would not know what to do with you.

53
00:03:10,500 --> 00:03:14,539
And it gets worse. Under the empire's criminal code, a death sentence

54
00:03:14,539 --> 00:03:18,579
usually needed a confession. And the law allowed torture to get one.

55
00:03:18,579 --> 00:03:22,619
So when the court needs a confession, guess who they send for.

56
00:03:22,919 --> 00:03:27,199
Not the judge. The judge has clean hands. That's what you're for.

57
00:03:27,500 --> 00:03:31,514
On the fifth of June, fifteen seventy-three, you perform your first execution.

58
00:03:31,514 --> 00:03:35,530
And you do something almost no executioner did.

59
00:03:35,830 --> 00:03:37,764
You write it down. And you'll keep writing for the next forty-five

60
00:03:37,764 --> 00:03:39,699
years.

61
00:03:40,000 --> 00:03:42,389
That diary survived. It's one of the reasons we know this life

62
00:03:42,389 --> 00:03:44,780
wasn't just in your head.

63
00:03:45,080 --> 00:03:48,830
At first, your entries are short. Just a name, a crime, and

64
00:03:48,830 --> 00:03:52,579
the punishment. But over the years, they get longer. You start writing

65
00:03:52,579 --> 00:03:56,329
who these people were, and what they did. Almost like you're trying

66
00:03:56,329 --> 00:04:00,079
to explain it. To someone. Maybe to yourself.

67
00:04:00,379 --> 00:04:04,349
A few years later, you land the big job. Master executioner of

68
00:04:04,349 --> 00:04:08,319
Nuremberg, one of the richest cities in the Holy Roman Empire.

69
00:04:08,620 --> 00:04:11,923
The city gives you a house. Not in town, of course. On

70
00:04:11,923 --> 00:04:15,226
a bridge over the river, slightly away from everyone. Locals called it

71
00:04:15,226 --> 00:04:18,529
the hangman's bridge.

72
00:04:18,829 --> 00:04:21,824
The pay is good. Better than most craftsmen. The city needs you,

73
00:04:21,824 --> 00:04:24,819
and they know nobody wants your job.

74
00:04:25,120 --> 00:04:28,805
You even get married and have seven kids. Your wife becomes the

75
00:04:28,805 --> 00:04:32,490
executioner's wife. You can guess how the neighbours take that.

76
00:04:32,790 --> 00:04:36,036
Even shopping is awkward. People don't want to touch your hands, your

77
00:04:36,036 --> 00:04:39,283
money, or your shadow. Imagine trying to buy bread from a baker

78
00:04:39,283 --> 00:04:42,529
who'd rather throw it at you.

79
00:04:42,829 --> 00:04:49,029
Your clients are thieves, robbers, murderers. Most are hanged. Some, by sword.

80
00:04:49,329 --> 00:04:52,409
And here's the thing about a sword execution. You get one swing.

81
00:04:52,409 --> 00:04:55,490
Miss, and the crowd turns on you.

82
00:04:55,790 --> 00:04:59,176
That's not a joke. If an execution went badly, an angry crowd

83
00:04:59,176 --> 00:05:02,563
could attack the executioner. You are basically doing live theatre, where the

84
00:05:02,563 --> 00:05:05,949
audience might kill you.

85
00:05:06,250 --> 00:05:09,926
Before an execution, the condemned person was often given a final meal.

86
00:05:09,926 --> 00:05:13,603
Sometimes a very good one. You may be the only person in

87
00:05:13,603 --> 00:05:17,279
town who sees that people about to die are still people.

88
00:05:17,579 --> 00:05:20,883
And look at your sword. It doesn't even have a sharp point.

89
00:05:20,883 --> 00:05:24,186
An executioner's sword wasn't built for fighting. It was built for exactly

90
00:05:24,186 --> 00:05:27,490
one thing. One clean swing.

91
00:05:27,790 --> 00:05:31,100
Over your career, your diary records hundreds of executions. And you write

92
00:05:31,100 --> 00:05:34,409
every one in the same calm, careful way.

93
00:05:34,709 --> 00:05:38,539
You also push for some changes. Around fifteen eighty, you help convince

94
00:05:38,539 --> 00:05:42,370
the city to stop drowning women as a punishment, and to use

95
00:05:42,370 --> 00:05:46,199
the sword instead. Which, in your world, counts as mercy.

96
00:05:46,500 --> 00:05:50,102
And here's a fact that will put you off your lunch. Executioners

97
00:05:50,102 --> 00:05:53,704
in German lands were known to sell healing remedies made from the

98
00:05:53,704 --> 00:05:57,307
bodies of the executed. Fat, skin, bones. People believed it was powerful

99
00:05:57,307 --> 00:06:00,909
medicine. And they paid well.

100
00:06:01,209 --> 00:06:04,370
Same townspeople. Won't shake your hand. Will absolutely buy a jar of

101
00:06:04,370 --> 00:06:07,529
something from you after dark.

102
00:06:07,829 --> 00:06:10,764
But honestly? The strangest part of your life isn't the killing. It's

103
00:06:10,764 --> 00:06:13,699
what you do on your days off.

104
00:06:14,000 --> 00:06:18,740
Now here's the twist nobody expects. Your other job? Doctor.

105
00:06:19,040 --> 00:06:22,856
Executioners knew bodies better than most doctors. So people secretly came to

106
00:06:22,856 --> 00:06:26,673
you to fix broken bones and wounds. By your own count, you

107
00:06:26,673 --> 00:06:30,490
treated around fifteen thousand patients.

108
00:06:30,790 --> 00:06:32,935
They'll let you heal them in private. Then pretend they don't know

109
00:06:32,935 --> 00:06:35,079
you on the street.

110
00:06:35,379 --> 00:06:38,666
You also reportedly never drink alcohol. In a century where everyone drinks

111
00:06:38,666 --> 00:06:41,953
beer for breakfast, the town's executioner is the most sober man in

112
00:06:41,953 --> 00:06:45,240
Nuremberg.

113
00:06:45,540 --> 00:06:49,400
Maybe it's discipline. Maybe it's because a drunk executioner is a terrible

114
00:06:49,400 --> 00:06:53,259
idea. Or maybe it's because you know that one bad day at

115
00:06:53,259 --> 00:06:57,120
work, for you, means a horror show for everyone watching.

116
00:06:57,420 --> 00:07:00,350
After about forty years, you retire. Your body is tired. Your name

117
00:07:00,350 --> 00:07:03,279
still isn't clean.

118
00:07:03,579 --> 00:07:06,259
So you do the one thing you've wanted since childhood. You write

119
00:07:06,259 --> 00:07:08,939
to the emperor and beg for your honour back. Not for you.

120
00:07:08,939 --> 00:07:11,620
For your kids.

121
00:07:11,920 --> 00:07:14,500
And in sixteen twenty-four, at seventy years old, the emperor actually says

122
00:07:14,500 --> 00:07:17,079
yes.

123
00:07:17,379 --> 00:07:21,414
After a lifetime of killing for the law, the law finally decides

124
00:07:21,414 --> 00:07:25,449
you are a respectable person. Your kids can finally live normal lives.

125
00:07:25,750 --> 00:07:29,273
Because remember. That's the whole point. You never wanted to be clean

126
00:07:29,273 --> 00:07:32,796
for yourself. You wanted your children to walk into a church, sit

127
00:07:32,796 --> 00:07:36,319
wherever they like, and have nobody move away.

128
00:07:36,620 --> 00:07:40,139
You die ten years later, in sixteen thirty-four. And people actually attend

129
00:07:40,139 --> 00:07:43,659
your funeral. Respectable ones.

130
00:07:43,959 --> 00:07:47,394
And your diary? It survives. Centuries later, it gets printed and translated

131
00:07:47,394 --> 00:07:50,829
into English. The man nobody would sit next to in church becomes

132
00:07:50,829 --> 00:07:54,264
one of the most famous executioners in history, because he wrote it

133
00:07:54,264 --> 00:07:57,699
all down.

134
00:07:58,000 --> 00:08:01,560
Historians still read your words today. Which is more than anyone can

135
00:08:01,560 --> 00:08:05,120
say for the people who moved away from you at church.

136
00:08:05,420 --> 00:08:08,403
So that's your life. Born into a normal family. Ruined because a

137
00:08:08,403 --> 00:08:11,386
prince needed a hangman. Shunned for doing the job your city paid

138
00:08:11,386 --> 00:08:14,370
you to do.

139
00:08:14,670 --> 00:08:16,956
And the people who refused to shake your hand at church were

140
00:08:16,956 --> 00:08:19,243
the same people knocking on your door at night to fix their

141
00:08:19,243 --> 00:08:21,529
broken arms.

142
00:08:21,829 --> 00:08:24,496
Thanks for living it with us. Next time, you're a plague doctor.

143
00:08:24,496 --> 00:08:27,163
Bird mask, terrible medicine, worst possible timing. Subscribe, and pick your next

144
00:08:27,163 --> 00:08:29,829
past life.
